<h3>Hegel’s life and world</h3>
<p>Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel was born in Stuttgart in 1770, the same year as Beethoven and Wordsworth — a fact that places him at the center of the Romantic era, a period of extraordinary intellectual and political upheaval. He grew up in the shadow of the American Revolution, studied theology in Tübingen during the French Revolution (which he and his classmates greeted with enthusiasm), and wrote his first great work, the <em>Phenomenology of Spirit</em>, in 1807 as Napoleon was occupying Jena. He reportedly watched Napoleon ride through the city and described him as “the world-soul on horseback” — a phrase that captures both his admiration and his entire philosophy of history in miniature.</p>
<p>His career followed an unusual arc. After completing his doctorate he spent years as a private tutor — educated, brilliant, and largely unknown — before finally securing a university position at Jena, which the Napoleonic invasion then promptly disrupted. He edited a newspaper in Bamberg, ran a preparatory school in Nuremberg for nearly a decade, published his <em>Science of Logic</em>, and eventually made his way to the University of Berlin in 1818, where he became the dominant figure in German philosophy until his death from cholera in 1831.</p>
<p>The world Hegel moved through was one in constant transformation: the old political orders of Europe were being dismantled and rebuilt; the Industrial Revolution was beginning to reshape daily life; national identities were crystallizing; and the question of what freedom actually means — for individuals, for peoples, for history itself — had become urgent in ways it had never quite been before. Hegel’s philosophy is, among other things, an attempt to make sense of all this from the inside.</p>
</div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-1" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-1" hidden=""><div>
<h3>From Hume’s skepticism to Kant’s response</h3>
<p>To understand what Hegel is doing, you need to understand the problem he was left with. That problem has two stages: Hume’s skepticism, and Kant’s attempt to answer it.</p>
<h4>Hume: the limits of reason</h4>
<p>Recall what Hume argued in Unit 3. Starting from the empiricist premise that all genuine knowledge traces back to sensory experience, he showed that several things philosophers had taken for granted — causation, the self, the reliability of induction — cannot actually be justified by reason. When you see one billiard ball strike another, you see succession, not necessary connection. When you expect the sun to rise tomorrow because it always has before, that expectation is a product of habit, not logic. Reason, for Hume, is powerful within its proper domain, but that domain is much smaller than most philosophers had assumed.</p>
<p>The most radical implication of Hume’s argument is this: the world as we understand it — with causes and effects, persisting objects, reliable regularities — may be a construction of the mind rather than a direct report of reality. Hume himself drew back from this precipice, concluding that habit and custom are good enough foundations for practical life even if they cannot be rationally justified. But he left philosophy in a genuinely difficult position.</p>

<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">💡</span><div class="callout__content">
