<h4>Kant: the mind structures experience</h4>
<p>Immanuel Kant, writing in the 1780s, famously described Hume as having woken him from his “dogmatic slumber.” He took Hume’s skepticism seriously — but he thought Hume had drawn the wrong conclusion from it.</p>
<p>Kant’s response was what he called the Copernican revolution in philosophy. Just as Copernicus had reoriented astronomy by placing the sun rather than the earth at the center of the solar system, Kant proposed reorienting epistemology by placing the <em>mind</em> rather than the <em>world</em> at the center of knowledge. The key move: instead of asking how the mind comes to match an independently existing world, Kant asked what the mind contributes to the structure of experience.</p>
<p>His answer was that the mind is not a passive receiver of sensations — a blank slate, as Locke had suggested — but an active organizer of experience. Before any particular experience is possible, the mind must already be structured with certain concepts: causation, substance, space, time. These are not learned from experience; they are the preconditions of experience. Hume had asked how we could derive the concept of causation from experience, and concluded we couldn’t. Kant agreed — but argued that this was because causation is not derived from experience; it is brought <em>to</em> experience by the mind. It is how the mind makes sense of the world, not a feature the world hands us.</p>
<p>This saves science: if causation is a structural feature of how minds work, then causal knowledge is genuinely reliable within the realm of possible experience. We can trust that the future will resemble the past, not because the universe guarantees it, but because our minds are constitutionally unable to experience the world any other way.</p>
<p>But it comes at a cost. Kant’s answer draws a sharp line between the world as we experience it — structured by the mind’s categories — and the world as it is in itself, independent of any mind. The latter, the <em>Ding an sich</em> (thing-in-itself), is permanently inaccessible to us. Reason is reliable within the bounds of possible experience, but it cannot reach beyond those bounds into ultimate reality. God, freedom, the soul — these remain beyond the reach of theoretical knowledge.</p>

