<h3>Why Kant’s answer wasn’t enough</h3>
<p>Hegel admired Kant. But he thought Kant had left philosophy with an intolerable problem: a permanent gap between mind and world, between how things appear to us and how they are in themselves. For Kant, we are confined to the world of appearances, constituted by the mind’s own categories. The thing-in-itself lies forever beyond reach.</p>
<p>Hegel’s response was radical: the gap is an illusion. The separation between mind and world, subject and object, thought and reality — these are not fixed divisions that philosophy must work around. They are tensions that philosophy must show to be overcome. The correct view, Hegel argued, is not that the mind imposes its structure on a fundamentally alien world, but that mind and world are aspects of a single, developing reality — and that reason is not just a tool we use to understand that reality, but is the very principle according to which it unfolds.</p>
<p>This is what he means by <strong>Absolute Idealism</strong>: the view that reality is, at its deepest level, rational — that the structure of thought and the structure of the world are not merely parallel or conveniently matched, but identical. The famous formula from the <em>Philosophy of Right</em> captures it: <em>What is rational is actual; and what is actual is rational.</em></p>
<p>This is a very strong claim, and it has a very strong implication: if reason and reality are ultimately the same, then the history of the world is not a random sequence of events but the unfolding of reason itself — a process by which reality comes to understand itself through human thought, culture, and civilization. History has a logic. It goes somewhere. And that somewhere is freedom.</p>
<div class="callout callout--connection"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">➡️</span><div class="callout__content">
<p>A note on “Idealism”</p>
<p>In everyday speech, “idealism” means something like naive optimism — believing things are better than they are. In philosophy, <em>idealism</em> is a position about the relationship between mind and reality: the view that reality is in some important sense mind-dependent or mind-like. This is not the claim that the world is just in our heads; it is the claim that the distinction between “in our heads” and “out there” is less sharp, or less fundamental, than we ordinarily assume.</p>
</div></div>
</div></div><div class="tabs__panel" role="tabpanel" id="tabs-0-panel-3" aria-labelledby="tabs-0-tab-3" hidden=""><div>
<h3>The hinge of the course</h3>
<p>Hegel sits at a turning point in this course — and in the history of philosophy. The first three units asked, in different ways, what we can know: Socrates showed that moral knowledge is harder to pin down than we assume; Descartes tried to establish certain knowledge from scratch; Hume showed that even Descartes’s foundations were shakier than he thought. Each of these was, broadly, an epistemological project — a project about the nature and limits of knowledge.</p>
<p>Hegel shifts the terrain. He does not primarily ask what we can know; he asks what <em>is</em> — what the nature of reality is, what history is, what freedom is. And his answer changes the questions that philosophy will ask for the rest of the nineteenth century and beyond.</p>
<p>The two philosophers who follow him in this course are both, in very different ways, his heirs. Marx accepts Hegel’s claim that history has a logic and moves toward freedom — but he inverts it. For Hegel, the motor of history is Spirit developing through ideas; for Marx, it is material conditions and class conflict. The grand historical narrative is preserved; its content is radically transformed. Sartre, meanwhile, arrives after the wreckage: the great systems of both Hegel and Marx have been tested by the twentieth century and found wanting, and the question is what freedom means in a world where no such guarantees are available.</p>

<div>
<p>The is/ought thread</p>
<p>Hume’s is/ought distinction — the observation that you cannot derive what <em>ought</em> to be from facts about what <em>is</em> — is directly relevant to Hegel. Hegel’s claim that the rational is real, and the real is rational, looks like exactly the move Hume warned against: deriving a normative conclusion (history is going somewhere good, freedom is its proper end) from a descriptive account of how history has developed. Keep this tension in view as you read. It will come back with Marx, and again with Sartre.</p>
</div>
